WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.840
How does heapify work? Repair

2
00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.880
one misplaced root by moving

3
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.540
it down a heap, choosing

4
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.940
the smaller child in a

5
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.600
min-heap. The child subtrees must

6
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.120
already be valid heaps.

7
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.060
A binary heap is a

8
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.220
complete binary tree stored in

9
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.480
an array. In zero-based storage,

10
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:19.960
the children of index i

11
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.400
are two i plus one

12
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.100
and two i plus two,

13
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.120
if those indices exist.

14
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.080
Here the root is nine,

15
00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.780
with children two and three.

16
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.960
Below them, four, five, six,

17
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:32.940
and seven already satisfy their

18
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.140
own parent relationships, so only

19
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.440
the root needs repair.

20
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.900
Compare the two children and

21
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.180
choose two, the smaller one.

22
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.720
Since nine is larger, swap

23
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.140
nine with two; the root

24
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.040
is now correct, but nine

25
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.020
may still violate order farther

26
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:49.340
down.

27
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.680
Nine now has children four

28
00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.740
and five. Choose four and

29
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.880
swap again; nine reaches a

30
00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:57.340
leaf, so the repair stops.

31
00:00:57.980 --> 00:00:59.920
The resulting array is two,

32
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.840
four, three, nine, five, six,

33
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.660
seven. It is a valid

34
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.360
min-heap, although it is not

35
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.600
a sorted array: siblings and

36
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.480
separate branches need no relative

37
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:09.880
order.

38
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.580
Why choose the smaller child?

39
00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.300
Moving it up makes it

40
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.840
no larger than either child

41
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.280
at the repaired position. Choosing

42
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.620
the larger child could leave

43
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.020
the smaller child below a

44
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.340
parent that is still too

45
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:22.760
large.

46
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.740
At each step, stop if

47
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.320
the parent is already no

48
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.200
larger than both valid children.

49
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.100
Otherwise descend one level; the

50
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.540
only remaining possible violation follows

51
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:35.720
the item moving down.

52
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.020
A node of subtree height

53
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.540
h can descend at most

54
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.740
h levels. Each level uses

55
00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.320
a constant number of comparisons

56
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.440
and one possible swap, giving

57
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.440
order h time and logarithmic

58
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.880
worst-case time at the root.

59
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.700
An iterative repair needs only

60
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.060
an index and a few

61
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.800
temporary words, so auxiliary space

62
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.440
is constant. A recursive repair

63
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.440
instead keeps up to logarithmically

64
00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.960
many active calls; those frames

65
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:03.920
count as space.

66
00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.060
Things to take note of.

67
00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.140
Check child bounds against the

68
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.740
active heap size before reading

69
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:11.340
them, and never invent a

70
00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.340
missing right child. A single

71
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.160
root heapify cannot repair an

72
00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.560
arbitrary unordered tree.

73
00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:18.938
Empty heaps have no root

74
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.440
to repair, and a singleton

75
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.480
is already a heap. Reverse

76
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.900
the comparisons and choose the

77
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.160
larger child for a max-heap.

78
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.500
Follow the violation down one

79
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:28.860
branch, and the whole repair

80
00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.540
becomes quite civilised.
